WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.234 --> 00:00:03.269
-Hi, Chelsea.
Welcome back to the show.

00:00:03.269 --> 00:00:05.070
You look great, bud.
Thanks so much

00:00:05.070 --> 00:00:08.374
for coming on the show,
and actually I want to thank you

00:00:08.374 --> 00:00:11.277
for coming on the show back
when we first started doing

00:00:11.277 --> 00:00:14.214
the show from home, from the --
where we started the quarantine,

00:00:14.214 --> 00:00:17.216
early in the quarantine.
-Yeah, you weren't in a good way

00:00:17.216 --> 00:00:19.219
when the quarantine started.
I remember.

00:00:19.219 --> 00:00:21.921
You were like -- I was like, "He
better get back to work soon."

00:00:21.921 --> 00:00:24.524
You were like, "How long am I
going to have to stay home?"

00:00:24.524 --> 00:00:26.692
-[ Laughs ]
-I mean, seriously.

00:00:26.692 --> 00:00:29.428
I remember getting off of our
Zoom and I was like,

00:00:29.428 --> 00:00:31.997
"Oh, he's not going
to like this.

00:00:31.997 --> 00:00:34.900
He's not liking quarantine
at all."

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:38.804
-That was challenging, man.
But I did -- now I look back

00:00:38.804 --> 00:00:41.774
and I go, "Oh, yeah, I think
it was fun, the home shows."

00:00:41.774 --> 00:00:43.242
But I don't remember
them really.

00:00:43.242 --> 00:00:45.444
It just like --
just happened so fast.

00:00:45.444 --> 00:00:47.480
It was just me --
it was Nancy and I.

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:49.282
There was no one --
could I say me and Nancy?

00:00:49.282 --> 00:00:50.817
Just me and Nancy.

00:00:50.817 --> 00:00:52.185
-It's just Nancy and me

00:00:52.185 --> 00:00:53.786
is really what you should be
saying.

00:00:53.786 --> 00:00:56.256
I mean, I hate to constantly
go over grammar with you.

00:00:56.256 --> 00:00:57.757
It's exhausting.

00:00:57.757 --> 00:00:59.726
-We just started the interview.
That's one.

00:00:59.726 --> 00:01:02.262
-I know.
-I get three strikes.

00:01:02.262 --> 00:01:04.330
-In this interview,
you get three strikes.

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:06.565
In our history --
-In our history, oh, forget it.

00:01:06.565 --> 00:01:08.134
I'm already -- oh, please.
Are you kidding me?

00:01:08.134 --> 00:01:09.969
I'm awful.
I'm bad with that.

00:01:09.969 --> 00:01:12.304
I've got to figure that out.
Next time I talk to you,

00:01:12.304 --> 00:01:15.208
you can test me on grammar
and see if I'm getting better.

00:01:15.208 --> 00:01:17.143
-All right, that sounds like
a fair shake. Thank you.

00:01:17.143 --> 00:01:19.578
-That sounds like a really
exciting part of the interview.

00:01:19.578 --> 00:01:21.247
-Yeah, it sounds like an episode
people are going to be

00:01:21.247 --> 00:01:23.283
dying to see.
[ Laughs ]

00:01:23.283 --> 00:01:25.551
-"Oh, did see the
Chelsea Grammar episode?"

00:01:25.551 --> 00:01:28.521
-Chelsea -- [ Laughs ]
Chelsea Grammar.

00:01:28.521 --> 00:01:30.256
I can team up with
Kelsey Grammer

00:01:30.256 --> 00:01:33.325
and we can both double team
you on your grammar.

00:01:33.325 --> 00:01:35.028
-Do you have your Halloween
costume yet?

00:01:35.028 --> 00:01:37.697
If not, you could be
Chelsea Grammar.

00:01:37.697 --> 00:01:39.832
-That's it.
-I'm just saying.

00:01:39.832 --> 00:01:41.934
Are you a Halloween person?
I feel like you were

00:01:41.934 --> 00:01:44.170
and now you're not.

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:45.538
-It's weird that you would
think that,

00:01:45.538 --> 00:01:47.073
but I'm not into dressing up.

00:01:47.073 --> 00:01:48.374
I feel like it's degrading.

00:01:48.374 --> 00:01:49.575
You know what I mean?
-Yeah.

00:01:49.575 --> 00:01:51.377
-I'm not into costumes.
You're a good sport,

00:01:51.377 --> 00:01:53.546
and I don't have a good sport
attitude.

00:01:53.546 --> 00:01:56.015
I'm kind of like a Scrooge when
it comes to people telling me

00:01:56.015 --> 00:01:58.084
what to do on a certain day
of the year.

00:01:58.084 --> 00:01:59.586
I take offense to that.

00:01:59.586 --> 00:02:04.323
[ Laughter ]

00:02:04.323 --> 00:02:07.493
It's like Christmas,
"No, don't tell me

00:02:07.493 --> 00:02:10.863
when I'm supposed to give
my friends and family gifts

00:02:10.863 --> 00:02:13.099
and celebrate something I don't
even believe in."

00:02:13.099 --> 00:02:15.234
-I agree with you about that.
Don't tell me what you want

00:02:15.234 --> 00:02:16.836
for Christmas.
That's the whole idea --

00:02:16.836 --> 00:02:19.072
No, I'm giving a gift.
I'm just giving

00:02:19.072 --> 00:02:22.475
and either you take it
or you go, 'Wow, okay.'"

00:02:22.475 --> 00:02:24.309
-Yeah.
-You get what you get.

00:02:24.309 --> 00:02:27.313
-This Christmas is going to be
different because like --

00:02:27.313 --> 00:02:29.782
you know, like you, I spend
a lot of time with my family.

00:02:29.782 --> 00:02:33.085
But I spent a lot of time,
they descended upon my house

00:02:33.085 --> 00:02:34.520
when quarantine started.

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:38.057
My sister, Simone,
who I used to like a lot more,

00:02:38.057 --> 00:02:40.159
and her three children.

00:02:40.159 --> 00:02:41.460
They lived with me
for two months.

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:44.230
And that was, you know,
wonderful to see my house

00:02:44.230 --> 00:02:45.864
being used in that way.

00:02:45.864 --> 00:02:48.234
But it was a lot of time with
children that I chose

00:02:48.234 --> 00:02:50.503
not to have on my own, you know?

00:02:50.503 --> 00:02:54.207
So it felt like, wait a second,
I made the right decisions

00:02:54.207 --> 00:02:56.441
in my life,
yet I'm being punished.

00:02:56.441 --> 00:02:58.578
And then over the summer,

00:02:58.578 --> 00:03:00.946
we went on like a two-week
family vacation

00:03:00.946 --> 00:03:03.249
with my entire family.
-Aww.

00:03:03.249 --> 00:03:05.151
-And with all my nieces
and nephews.

00:03:05.151 --> 00:03:07.920
So for Christmas, they're like,
"What's the plan for Christmas?"

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:09.388
And we usually go for Canada.

00:03:09.388 --> 00:03:11.390
I'm like, "We can't get into
Canada this year

00:03:11.390 --> 00:03:13.225
because we're pariahs.

00:03:13.225 --> 00:03:15.194
We can't go skiing."

00:03:15.194 --> 00:03:16.695
I said, "So I guess everyone's

00:03:16.695 --> 00:03:18.331
on their own this year
for COVID."

00:03:18.331 --> 00:03:20.800
You know, we already had the
vacation at Martha's Vineyard.

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:23.569
And my brother actually had
the audacity to say to me,

00:03:23.569 --> 00:03:26.839
"What about you coming East and
we all go skiing in Vermont?"

00:03:26.839 --> 00:03:29.108
I go, "I don't think you're
getting the message.

00:03:29.108 --> 00:03:32.011
I'm not celebrating Christmas
with you guys, okay?

00:03:32.011 --> 00:03:33.913
I want to be alone."
-No.

00:03:33.913 --> 00:03:36.882
You're like, "I don't go to you,
we go to a place.

00:03:36.882 --> 00:03:38.918
But I'm not gonna go --
what are we --"

00:03:38.918 --> 00:03:40.886
I think you're going to go.

00:03:40.886 --> 00:03:42.789
-I'm not going to Vermont.
I'm not.

00:03:42.789 --> 00:03:45.057
-Turns out I don't know you
at all.

00:03:45.057 --> 00:03:46.625
-No, you don't.
It doesn't seem like --

00:03:46.625 --> 00:03:49.595
The more we talk, the less it
seems like you know.

00:03:49.595 --> 00:03:53.166
-I've read the books, I've seen
the specials, I watch the show.

00:03:53.166 --> 00:03:55.634
I've watched -- I've seen
everything, the whole --

00:03:55.634 --> 00:03:57.002
the evolution.

00:03:57.002 --> 00:03:59.238
I'm not segueing yet, but the
evolution of your career.

00:03:59.238 --> 00:04:01.140
I've been there.
We're getting close.

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:03.609
-You have.
-I have the long setup.

00:04:03.609 --> 00:04:04.777
So I say "evolution,"

00:04:04.777 --> 00:04:07.012
then eventually we'll get
to the setup.

00:04:07.012 --> 00:04:08.714
But not yet.
-Okay, should I just wait here?

00:04:08.714 --> 00:04:11.217
Should I just wait here?
-Yes.

00:04:11.217 --> 00:04:12.785
You should just wait there.
I was thinking,

00:04:12.785 --> 00:04:15.555
is my grammar
correct on that?

00:04:15.555 --> 00:04:20.026
You're very open about how
therapy has helped you

00:04:20.026 --> 00:04:23.129
become a more patient person
in some ways.

00:04:23.129 --> 00:04:25.298
Is that something that you
have to work on

00:04:25.298 --> 00:04:28.301
all the time and still do?
-Well, yes.

00:04:28.301 --> 00:04:30.303
I mean, for sure.
If I could learn

00:04:30.303 --> 00:04:33.105
how to get a handle on
my patience, anyone can.

00:04:33.105 --> 00:04:36.943
And you know, you think you're
doing well, like I -- you know,

00:04:36.943 --> 00:04:40.046
I'm making a lot of
improvements, I'm less reactive.

00:04:40.046 --> 00:04:41.814
But when I came home
this last trip,

00:04:41.814 --> 00:04:43.349
I had been gone
for like two months.

00:04:43.349 --> 00:04:45.851
I went and shot my special,
I went on my family vacation,

00:04:45.851 --> 00:04:48.554
then I rented a house in Maine
for a couple weeks.

00:04:48.554 --> 00:04:52.992
And I came home and my assistant
had had all the decks

00:04:52.992 --> 00:04:56.128
painted black,
dark, dark black.

00:04:56.128 --> 00:04:59.966
Paint on my wood, my Ipe wood,
and my house.

00:04:59.966 --> 00:05:02.868
And this is -- and I think, you
know, like "Oh, I'm reasonable.

00:05:02.868 --> 00:05:04.603
I'm a rational person."

00:05:04.603 --> 00:05:07.506
I walked outside, saw my --
and my decks, and my house,

00:05:07.506 --> 00:05:08.640
and compass, everything.

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:10.376
And I went off.

00:05:10.376 --> 00:05:12.277
I was like, "How could you think

00:05:12.277 --> 00:05:15.114
that I would think that
this would look good?

00:05:15.114 --> 00:05:17.850
Black paint!" I go,
"It's too hot to even walk on.

00:05:17.850 --> 00:05:21.020
The dogs are going to scald
their little baby paws."

00:05:21.020 --> 00:05:22.421
And then --
I couldn't believe it.

00:05:22.421 --> 00:05:24.189
I go, "How could you think
I would like paint?"

00:05:24.189 --> 00:05:25.824
-Yes.
-"How could you paint wood?

00:05:25.824 --> 00:05:27.292
And how could paint it
this color?"

00:05:27.292 --> 00:05:29.529
-"Am I a person that looks like
they like painted wood?

00:05:29.529 --> 00:05:30.997
Don't you know me?"
-Yeah, and I looked --

00:05:30.997 --> 00:05:33.599
And I'm like -- oh --
as soon as it's out

00:05:33.599 --> 00:05:35.267
and I'm screaming and saying
these words

00:05:35.267 --> 00:05:37.703
and being such a small person
and I'm worried about

00:05:37.703 --> 00:05:39.839
my stupid decks when we're
in the middle of a pandemic,

00:05:39.839 --> 00:05:41.941
I was like, "Shut up,
shut up, shut up,

00:05:41.941 --> 00:05:43.676
you're being a bitch.
Shut up, shut up.

00:05:43.676 --> 00:05:45.478
Stop talking!
Stop talking."

00:05:45.478 --> 00:05:48.681
And I'm like, you think you're
all, you know, fixed or better

00:05:48.681 --> 00:05:50.182
and then you have a slip-up
like that,

00:05:50.182 --> 00:05:51.717
and that was really
embarrassing.

00:05:51.717 --> 00:05:53.185
I had to apologize
to my assistant

00:05:53.185 --> 00:05:55.588
'cause I had an outburst like
I hadn't had in years.

00:05:55.588 --> 00:05:57.089
So it's a work in progress.

00:05:57.089 --> 00:05:58.524
-Yeah, I have to call
your therapist,

00:05:58.524 --> 00:06:01.594
'cause I have the same problem.

00:06:01.594 --> 00:06:04.263
Did you -- let's get into
something that's less stressful.

00:06:04.263 --> 00:06:06.399
Did you watch the debate
last night?

00:06:06.399 --> 00:06:08.167
[ Light laughter ]

00:06:08.167 --> 00:06:10.068
-You know what?
I took myself out to dinner

00:06:10.068 --> 00:06:12.137
instead of watching the debate,

00:06:12.137 --> 00:06:14.273
because I've heard that idiot
talk for four years

00:06:14.273 --> 00:06:16.275
and I don't need to hear him
say another word.

00:06:16.275 --> 00:06:17.743
-[ Laughs ]

00:06:17.743 --> 00:06:19.412
-I mean, seriously.
My blood pressure,

00:06:19.412 --> 00:06:20.946
you can already tell
when I get enervated.

00:06:20.946 --> 00:06:22.748
It's not good for anybody.
-No.

00:06:22.748 --> 00:06:24.583
-So I can't watch him talk.
No, thanks.

00:06:24.583 --> 00:06:26.018
-I think --
-And you know --

00:06:26.018 --> 00:06:27.152
-Go.

00:06:27.152 --> 00:06:28.488
-You heard about my
ex-boyfriend, right?

00:06:28.488 --> 00:06:32.324
50 Cent and his support
of Donald Trump?

00:06:32.324 --> 00:06:34.026
How he tweeted about it.
-Yeah, what's going on?

00:06:34.026 --> 00:06:36.095
I saw your tweets and I go,
"Wait, what's happening?"

00:06:36.095 --> 00:06:38.731
'Cause you said he was your
favorite ex-boyfriend.

00:06:38.731 --> 00:06:42.768
And then he -- what does he do?
He's supporting Trump?

00:06:42.768 --> 00:06:45.270
-He says he doesn't want to pay
62% of taxes,

00:06:45.270 --> 00:06:47.339
which by the way, isn't
a plan of Joe Biden's.

00:06:47.339 --> 00:06:49.041
That's a lie.

00:06:49.041 --> 00:06:51.210
So he doesn't want to pay
62% of taxes

00:06:51.210 --> 00:06:55.414
because he doesn't want to go
from being 50 Cent to 20 Cent.

00:06:55.414 --> 00:06:59.952
And I -- and I had to remind him
that he was a black person,

00:06:59.952 --> 00:07:01.754
so he can't vote
for Donald Trump

00:07:01.754 --> 00:07:03.289
and that he shouldn't be
influencing

00:07:03.289 --> 00:07:06.792
an entire swath of people
who may listen to him

00:07:06.792 --> 00:07:09.762
because he's worried about
his own personal pocketbook.

00:07:09.762 --> 00:07:14.333
So I haven't heard back from him
yet, but I am willing to,

00:07:14.333 --> 00:07:16.768
you know, seal the deal
in more ways than one

00:07:16.768 --> 00:07:18.337
if he changes his mind

00:07:18.337 --> 00:07:20.406
and publicly denounces
Donald Trump.

00:07:20.406 --> 00:07:22.140
I might be willing to go
for another spin,

00:07:22.140 --> 00:07:24.043
if you know what
I'm talking about.

